This GitHub repository contains a script for automating network interface monitoring and configuration on Cisco devices using Tcl scripting within the Embedded Event Manager (EEM) environment.

Features
Interface Status Monitoring: The script checks the status of a specified network interface and logs events.
Automatic Speed and Duplex Configuration: If the interface speed or duplex settings are not set to auto, the script can automatically reconfigure them.
Phone Detection via LLDP: Detects phones connected to the interface using LLDP and ensures proper interface settings.
Clear Interface Counters: Clears counters on the interface based on its status (up, down, or administratively down).
Shutdown Unused Interfaces: Automatically shuts down interfaces that haven't been used in a specified amount of time.
Script Overview
The script takes an interface name as an argument and performs the following steps:

Trigger Log: Logs an EEM trigger event.
Interface Status Check:
If the interface is up, it checks for connected phones using LLDP.
If no phone is detected, it ensures that the speed and duplex settings are set to auto.
If the interface is down, it checks the last input time and shuts down the interface if it hasn't been used in a long time.
If the interface is administratively down, it logs the event.
Clearing Counters: Clears interface counters after processing.
Prerequisites
Cisco device with Embedded Event Manager (EEM) enabled.
Access to the Cisco CLI for script deployment.
Basic knowledge of Tcl scripting.

Usage
Clone the repository to your local machine:

git clone https://github.com/yourusername/network-automation.git
cd network-automation
Modify the script as necessary to fit your environment.
Deploy the script on a Cisco device with EEM enabled.
Trigger the script by specifying the interface to be monitored.
